{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a13f09cbacf954801","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CeZTyCbNHZaTNEHPpVEWY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V63jSE8kL3hgEdDyZMF1ss","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"fork","name":"cg-contraction","description":"Build CG contraction module","prompt":"Write the CG contraction module at `/Users/blake/Desktop/MIT/OpenWave/M8.5C2_BUILD/build/cg_contraction.py`.\n\nThis implements the SECOND route for computing P_N[|ψ|²ψ] — purely in coefficient space via Clebsch-Gordan coefficients, as required by § 4.3 of the protocol.\n\nThe function signature:\n```python\ndef cg_project_cubic(coeffs, basis_obj, N, rho='R0'):\n    \"\"\"Compute P_N[|ψ|²ψ] via CG contraction in coefficient space.\"\"\"\n```\n\nThe algorithm must NOT go through pointwise synthesis/analysis. Instead:\n1. Compute standard SU(2) CG coefficients ⟨j₁ m₁; j₂ m₂ | J M⟩\n2. Use CG to decompose pointwise products of representation matrix elements\n3. Factor the quartic coupling integral through CG coefficients\n\nKey formula (quartic integral of Wigner D-matrices on SU(2)):\n∫ D̄^{j₁}_{m₁m₁'} D^{j₃}_{m₃m₃'} D̄^{j₂}_{m₂m₂'} D^{j₄}_{m₄m₄'} dg\n= δ_{m₁+m₂,m₃+m₄} δ_{m₁'+m₂',m₃'+m₄'} × (-1)^{m₂-m₂'+m₃-m₃'}\n  × Σ_J (1/(2J+1)) \n    × ⟨j₁,-m₁; j₃,m₃|J, m₃-m₁⟩ ⟨j₁,-m₁'; j₃,m₃'|J, m₃'-m₁'⟩  \n    × ⟨j₂,-m₂; j₄,m₄|J, m₁-m₃⟩ ⟨j₂,-m₂'; j₄,m₄'|J, m₁'-m₃'⟩\n\nBut this is for the standard WEIGHT basis D-matrices. Our code uses UNITARIZED representation π^u_n which differs by:\nπ^u_n[a,b] ↔ D^{n/2}_{n/2-a, n/2-b} × (unitarization)\n\nThe unitarization is: π^u = G^{1/2} π^m G^{-1/2} where G = diag(g_0,...,g_n), g_k = k!(n-k)!/n!\n\nSo the quartic integral for π^u involves unitarization factors. Concretely:\n∫ π̄^u_{n₁}[a₁,b₁] π̄^u_{n₂}[a₂,b₂] π^u_{n₃}[a₃,b₃] π^u_{n₄}[a₄,b₄] dμ\n= √(g^{n₁}_{a₁} g^{n₂}_{a₂}) / √(g^{n₃}_{a₃} g^{n₄}_{a₄}) × √(g^{n₃}_{b₃} g^{n₄}_{b₄}) / √(g^{n₁}_{b₁} g^{n₂}_{b₂})\n  × (quartic integral for standard D with the weight conversion m_k = n_k/2 - a_k)\n\nRecommended TWO-PASS algorithm for efficiency:\nPass 1 - Compute density coefficients: ρ_l[A,J] = Σ_{n₂,n₃} Σ_{...} c̄_{n₂,...} c_{n₃,...} × CG(n₂,n₃→l)\nPass 2 - Multiply density × field: result_{n₁,...} = Σ_l Σ_{n₄} ρ_l × c_{n₄,...} × CG(l,n₄→n₁)\n\nDependencies: imports from `build.group` (pi_n_unitary, multiplicity, DIMS, _sym_gram), `build.sections` (build_basis_object, total_modes).\n\nVERIFICATION: After writing the module, test it against the fast transform route:\n```python\nfrom build.galerkin import project_cubic\nfrom build.cg_contraction import cg_project_cubic\n# They should agree to ~1e-12\n```\n\nTest at N=4, 8, 12 for R0.\n\nFor the CG coefficient computation, you can use the standard Racah formula or implement the recursion. A simple explicit formula:\n⟨j₁ m₁; j₂ m₂ | J M⟩ = δ_{M,m₁+m₂} × √(...) × Σ_s (-1)^s / (s! × ...) \nusing the standard sum formula from angular momentum theory.\n\nNOTE: the multiplicity in the SU(2) Clebsch-Gordan is always 1 (no outer multiplicity).\n\nWrite the file and verify."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":373,"cache_read_input_tokens":152932,"output_tokens":25787,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":24315},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":373},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":25787,"cache_read_input_tokens":152932,"cache_creation_input_tokens":373,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":373,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CeZTyBg4DWkeSEXzV9jPH","type":"assistant","uuid":"248a8d34-4fb5-4f4c-ae57-5eba11c3adaa","timestamp":"2026-08-30T19:20:39.788Z","effort":"high","user